"""dotdiw -- workflow discovery for a project.

Everything the tracker does, under one name, plus the part no tracker ships:
when work starts, the procedure that applies surfaces on its own.

Four capabilities have to work in a project. A file in the repository covers
declaration, the tracker covers management, git covers sharing, and this covers
discovery. It owns the moment work starts -- `dotdiw create` -- and hands
everything else to the tracker unchanged, so no task ever requires typing the
tracker's own name.

The tracker is behind a door of its own. An adapter answers the eleven operations
of the tracker interface, chosen per request, else per workspace, else the
shipped default. Two ship here: local, one sqlite file under the home and
the default for a new adoption, and beads, which runs bd, whose output names
itself in compiled-in hints, so its stream is rewritten on the way out.
Nothing outside the beads adapter names the binary.

Formulas live anywhere in the tree, next to whatever they are about. A
formula without a suffix is in local storage, the file system, and is found
there whether or not it is committed; it reaches the rest of the project when
it is committed. A formula management provider, registered under a name,
serves its formulas with that name as their suffix.

The file is shaped by the promise it keeps. operation-idl.json, derived from
poa/product-model.yaml, names every operation. Each is one function below, under
the IDL's name, taking a `request` dict and returning a dict with `status`,
`summary` and `exitCode`, except the ten of the tracker interface, which an
adapter answers. Every operation is invoked through
one door, `invoke`, whose table is generated from the IDL: the request is
held to its type on the way in, the response to its own on the way out, and
the witness log is appended there, from the response. Orchestrations call
step operations through the door, and nothing else is shared between
operations except the infrastructure at the top: running git, the FTS
storage, file parsing, the workspace, the index held for one operation, the
witness log's append. promise_bindings.py re-exports
the operations under the IDL's names, and test_promise.py holds each body to
the calls the IDL declares for it. `main()` is the command line's binding:
it calls only the door, shows what each operation returned as it returns,
and decides nothing about meaning. No body prints.

Silence the hints with DOTDIW_NO_HINTS=1. Witness what happens, locally and
only here, with DOTDIW_WITNESS=1 or by creating ~/.dotdiw/<key>/witness.jsonl.
"""

